//? Title: Attention: Soft Lookup, Scaling and Linear-Time Variants
//? Subtitle: Scaled dot-product, additive and multi-head attention checked against loops; why the scores are divided by √d, with the large-n entropy law of the softmax; masks and sliding windows; kernel (linear) attention and FAVOR+ random features; a lookup task and digits as sequences, using datamunge's nn::scaled_dot_product_attention, nn::multi_head_attention, nn::kernel_attention and nn::positive_random_features
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Attention returns a weighted average of value vectors, with weights given by a softmax over query–key scores. This report checks datamunge's scaled dot-product, additive and multi-head
attention against hand-written loops, to about \(10^{-16}\). It explains the \(1/\sqrt d\) scale: the scores of random \(d\)-dimensional vectors have variance \(d\), and a large-\(n\) law,
\(H\approx\log n-\sigma^2/2\), gives the softmax's entropy at score scale \(\sigma\), collapsing at \(\sigma=\sqrt{2\log n}\). The law fits 64 keys to 5% for \(\sigma\le1.5\) and 4096 keys to 0.1% up to \(\sigma=1.5\).
It then derives kernel (linear) attention and the FAVOR+ random-feature estimator of the softmax kernel, confirms that the estimator is unbiased and that its error falls at the
\(m^{-1/2}\) rate, and times the variants. Kernel attention scales linearly in sequence length and is more than 40 times faster than plain softmax attention at 4096 tokens. In a lookup task, softmax and FAVOR+ attention learn a sharp pointer
to the matching key, while elu-kernel attention does not. On digits read as sequences of pixel rows, one attention block lifts test accuracy from 49% to 86%–90%. The library gained `nn::kernel_attention`, `nn::elu_feature_map`,
`nn::positive_random_features`, `nn::orthogonal_gaussian_projection`, `nn::local_keep_mask` and `nn::attention_entropy`.
)

\toc

> The problem

A layer that mixes information across positions must decide, for each position, which other positions matter. Attention makes that decision with data: each query scores every key, a softmax
turns the scores into weights, and the output is the weighted average of the values (Bahdanau et al., 2015; Vaswani et al., 2017). It is a differentiable dictionary lookup. Three questions follow.
How should the scores be scaled so that the softmax neither averages everything nor picks a single key at random? How do masks restrict what each query sees? And since the weights form
a \(T_q\times T_k\) matrix, can the quadratic cost in sequence length be avoided without losing what makes attention work?

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.2_reusable_layer_library/attention.cpp`. That example checked the attention functions against loops, tabulated the
effect of the \(1/\sqrt d\) scale and trained a key–value lookup. All of that is kept, joined by the theory of the scale, linear-time variants and a dataset.

> The method

>> Scaled dot-product attention and its gradient

For queries \(Q\in\mathbb R^{T_q\times d}\), keys \(K\in\mathbb R^{T_k\times d}\) and values \(V\in\mathbb R^{T_k\times d_v}\),

$$
\operatorname{Attention}(Q,K,V)=\operatorname{softmax}\!\Big(\frac{QK^\top}{\sqrt d}+M\Big)V,
$$
\alttext(Attention of Q, K, V equals the softmax of Q K transpose over root d plus M, times V.)

with the softmax taken over keys and \(M\) an additive mask, 0 where a key is visible and \(-\infty\) where it is not. `nn::scaled_dot_product_attention` computes it, either by materialising
the weights or by a fused kernel that runs an online softmax over blocks of keys and never holds the \(T_q\times T_k\) matrix (Dao et al., 2022). For one query with scores \(s\) and weights \(\alpha=\operatorname{softmax}(s)\),
\(\partial\alpha_j/\partial s_i=\alpha_j(\delta_{ij}-\alpha_i)\). The Jacobian is \(J=\operatorname{diag}(\alpha)-\alpha\alpha^\top\), so a loss \(L=\sum_j\alpha_jr_j\) has gradient \(\partial L/\partial s=\alpha\odot(r-\alpha\cdot r)\), which sums to zero because
adding a constant to every score changes nothing. \(J\) vanishes at both extremes. If \(\alpha\) is uniform over \(n\) keys, \(\lVert J\rVert_F=\sqrt{n-1}/n\), and if \(\alpha\) is one-hot, \(J=0\). Between them, two equal weights give \(\lVert J\rVert_F=1/2\).

*Additive attention* (Bahdanau et al., 2015) scores with a small network instead, \(s_t=v^\top\tanh(W_qq+W_kk_t)\). *Multi-head attention* (Vaswani et al., 2017) projects the input with
\(W_Q,W_K,W_V\in\mathbb R^{D\times D}\), splits each projection into \(h\) heads of \(D/h\) columns, attends within each head and mixes the concatenated heads with \(W_O\). That is \(4D^2\) parameters, whatever \(h\) is.

>> Why divide by \(\sqrt d\)

If \(q\) and \(k\) have independent standard-normal entries, \(q\cdot k=\sum_{a=1}^dq_ak_a\) is a sum of \(d\) terms of variance 1. Its variance is \(d\), and dividing by \(\sqrt d\) restores 1. Given the query, the
scores against \(n\) random keys are independent \(\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2)\) with \(\sigma^2=\lVert q\rVert^2/d\approx1\). How peaked is the softmax of \(n\) independent \(\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2)\) scores \(s_j=\sigma z_j\)? Its entropy is
\(H=\log Z-\sum_j\alpha_js_j\) with \(Z=\sum_je^{s_j}\). For large \(n\) the law of large numbers gives \(Z\approx n\,\mathbb E e^{\sigma z}=ne^{\sigma^2/2}\). The weighted mean of the scores tends to
\(\mathbb E[\sigma z\,e^{\sigma z}]/\mathbb E[e^{\sigma z}]=\sigma^2\), by Stein's identity \(\mathbb E[zg(z)]=\mathbb E[g'(z)]\) (Stein, 1981). Hence

$$
H\approx\log n+\frac{\sigma^2}{2}-\sigma^2=\log n-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}.
$$
\alttext(H is approximately log n plus sigma squared over 2 minus sigma squared, which equals log n minus sigma squared over 2.)

The argument needs the exponentially tilted distribution, which is centred at \(z=\sigma\), to lie inside the sample, whose maximum is near \(\sqrt{2\log n}\). At \(\sigma=\sqrt{2\log n}\) the formula reaches zero,
and beyond it the weight condenses on the few largest scores. This is the freezing transition of Derrida's random energy model (Derrida, 1981). Unscaled scores have \(\sigma=\sqrt d\), so for 64 keys any
\(d\) above \(2\log64=8.3\) puts attention past the transition at initialisation.

>> Masks and sliding windows

A *keep* mask removes keys from the softmax. A causal mask lets query \(i\) see keys \(j\le i\). A padding mask hides positions past each sequence's length. `nn::local_keep_mask(Tq, Tk, w, causal)` keeps a
window: the current key and the \(w-1\) before it (Jiang et al., 2023), or every key within \(w-1\) on either side (Beltagy et al., 2020). Stacking \(L\) windowed layers reaches \(L(w-1)\) positions back,
at a cost linear in the sequence length. `nn::attention_entropy` measures how spread out each row of weights is.

>> Kernel attention and random features

The unnormalised weight \(\exp(q\cdot k/\sqrt d)\) is a kernel. If it were an inner product of feature maps, \(\kappa(q,k)=\varphi(q)\cdot\varphi(k)\) with \(\varphi:\mathbb R^d\to\mathbb R^m\), the sum over keys could be done once for all queries:

$$
\text{out}_i=\frac{\sum_j\varphi(q_i)^\top\varphi(k_j)\,v_j}{\sum_j\varphi(q_i)^\top\varphi(k_j)}
=\frac{\varphi(q_i)^\top S}{\varphi(q_i)^\top z},\qquad S=\sum_j\varphi(k_j)v_j^\top,\quad z=\sum_j\varphi(k_j).
$$
\alttext(Output i equals the sum over j of phi of q i transpose phi of k j times v j, divided by the sum over j of phi of q i transpose phi of k j. This equals phi of q i transpose S over phi of q i transpose z, where S is the sum over j of phi of k j times v j transpose, and z is the sum over j of phi of k j.)

That costs \(O((T_q+T_k)\,m\,d_v)\) instead of \(O(T_qT_kd)\). The causal version replaces \(S\) and \(z\) by prefix sums (Katharopoulos et al., 2020). `nn::kernel_attention(phi_q, phi_k, v, causal)` implements both.
Katharopoulos et al. used \(\varphi(x)=\mathrm{elu}(x)+1\) (`nn::elu_feature_map`), which defines a different kernel.

The softmax kernel itself has an unbiased random feature map. For \(w\sim\mathcal N(0,I_d)\), the Gaussian moment-generating function gives \(\mathbb E\,e^{w^\top u}=e^{\lVert u\rVert^2/2}\). So with
\(\varphi_w(x)=\exp(w^\top x-\lVert x\rVert^2/2)\),

$$
\mathbb E\big[\varphi_w(x)\varphi_w(y)\big]=e^{-(\lVert x\rVert^2+\lVert y\rVert^2)/2}\,\mathbb E\,e^{w^\top(x+y)}
=e^{-(\lVert x\rVert^2+\lVert y\rVert^2)/2+\lVert x+y\rVert^2/2}=e^{x\cdot y}.
$$
\alttext(The expected value of phi w of x times phi w of y equals e to the minus norm x squared plus norm y squared over 2, times the expected value of e to the w transpose x plus y. This equals e to the minus norm x squared plus norm y squared over 2 plus norm x plus y squared over 2, which equals e to the x dot y.)

Averaging \(m\) independent features, \(\varphi(x)=m^{-1/2}(\varphi_{w_1}(x),\dots,\varphi_{w_m}(x))\), estimates \(e^{x\cdot y}\) without bias. Its variance follows from the same identity applied to \(2(x+y)\):
\(\operatorname{Var}=e^{2x\cdot y}(e^{\lVert x+y\rVert^2}-1)/m\). The relative error therefore falls like \(m^{-1/2}\), with a constant that grows quickly with the norms. Every feature is positive, so the estimated weights stay
positive, unlike trigonometric random features. This is FAVOR+ (Choromanski et al., 2021), implemented by `nn::positive_random_features(x, w, s)`, which applies the softmax scale through
\(x'=\sqrt s\,x\). Drawing the rows of \(w\) orthogonal in blocks of \(d\), with chi-distributed lengths (`nn::orthogonal_gaussian_projection`), keeps each row marginally Gaussian, so the estimate stays
unbiased, and makes the features less correlated (Yu et al., 2016).

> Simple examples

>> Attention against loops

The program below checks plain, causal and windowed scaled dot-product attention, additive attention and multi-head attention against loops written from the definitions. It also
checks the softmax gradient against the closed form and draws one random sequence's attention weights under four masks.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/attention_masks.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using Rows = std::vector<std::vector<double>>;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

// softmax(q k^T / sqrt(d) + mask) v for one example, by loops; keep(i, j) says whether key j is visible.
template <class Keep>
void attend(const Rows& q, const Rows& k, const Rows& v, Keep keep, Rows& w, Rows& out) {
  const std::size_t Tq = q.size(), Tk = k.size(), d = q[0].size(), dv = v[0].size();
  w.assign(Tq, std::vector<double>(Tk, 0)), out.assign(Tq, std::vector<double>(dv, 0));
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < Tq; ++i) {
    std::vector<double> sc(Tk, -1e300);
    double mx = -1e300, z = 0;
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < Tk; ++j)
      if (keep(i, j)) {
        sc[j] = 0;
        for (std::size_t a = 0; a < d; ++a) sc[j] += q[i][a] * k[j][a];
        sc[j] /= std::sqrt(double(d)), mx = std::max(mx, sc[j]);
      }
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < Tk; ++j) if (keep(i, j)) z += std::exp(sc[j] - mx);
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < Tk; ++j) {
      w[i][j] = keep(i, j) ? std::exp(sc[j] - mx) / z : 0;
      for (std::size_t a = 0; a < dv; ++a) out[i][a] += w[i][j] * v[j][a];
    }
  }
}
Rows rows(const Tensor& t) {  // [T, d]
  const auto T = t.size(-2), d = t.size(-1);
  const auto v = t.contiguous().to_vector<double>();
  Rows r(T, std::vector<double>(d));
  for (int i = 0; i < T; ++i) for (int j = 0; j < d; ++j) r[i][j] = v[i * d + j];
  return r;
}
double maxdiff(const Rows& a, const Tensor& b) {
  const Rows rb = rows(b);
  double m = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) for (std::size_t j = 0; j < a[i].size(); ++j) m = std::max(m, std::abs(a[i][j] - rb[i][j]));
  return m;
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  nn::Generator gen(1);
  // 1. Scaled dot-product attention, q/k [1, 4, 8], v [1, 4, 3], against loops: plain, causal, and the fused kernel.
  const Tensor q = nn::normal({1, 4, 8}, 0, 1, gen, F64), k = nn::normal({1, 4, 8}, 0, 1, gen, F64), v = nn::normal({1, 4, 3}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
  std::print("| case | max \\|weights − loops\\| | max \\|output − loops\\| | weights (rows = queries) |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for (int c = 0; c < 3; ++c) {
    nn::AttentionOptions o{.need_weights = true};
    if (c == 1) o.causal = true;
    if (c == 2) o.mask = nn::local_keep_mask(4, 4, 2);
    const auto a = nn::scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, o);
    Rows w, out;
    attend(rows(nn::squeeze(q, {0})), rows(nn::squeeze(k, {0})), rows(nn::squeeze(v, {0})),
           [c](std::size_t i, std::size_t j) { return c == 0 || (j <= i && (c == 1 || i - j < 2)); }, w, out);
    std::string ws;
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
      ws += i ? "; " : "";
      for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j) ws += std::format("{}{:.3f}", j ? " " : "", w[i][j]);
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.1e} | {:.1e} | {} |\n", c == 0 ? "plain" : c == 1 ? "causal" : "local, window 2", maxdiff(w, nn::squeeze(a.weights, {0})),
               maxdiff(out, nn::squeeze(a.output, {0})), ws);
  }
  nn::AttentionOptions fused{.causal = true, .impl = nn::AttentionImpl::Fused};
  nn::AttentionOptions plainc{.causal = true, .impl = nn::AttentionImpl::Plain};
  std::print("\nFused causal kernel against the plain path: max difference {:.1e}.\n",
             nn::max(nn::abs(nn::scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, fused).output - nn::scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, plainc).output)).item());

  // 2. Additive (Bahdanau) attention: score_t = v^T tanh(Wq q + Wk k_t).
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(3, F64);
    const Tensor qa = nn::normal({2, 5}, 0, 1, gen, F64), ka = nn::normal({2, 6, 3}, 0, 1, gen, F64), va = nn::normal({2, 6, 2}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    const auto a = nn::additive_attention(p, "add", qa, ka, va, 4);
    const Tensor Wq = p.at("add/Wq"), Wk = p.at("add/Wk"), vec = p.at("add/v");
    double dw = 0, dout = 0;
    for (int n = 0; n < 2; ++n) {
      std::vector<double> sc(6);
      for (int t = 0; t < 6; ++t)
        for (int u = 0; u < 4; ++u) {
          double h = 0;
          for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) h += qa.at({n, i}) * Wq.at({i, u});
          for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) h += ka.at({n, t, i}) * Wk.at({i, u});
          sc[t] += std::tanh(h) * vec.at({u, 0});
        }
      const double mx = std::ranges::max(sc);
      double z = 0;
      for (double s : sc) z += std::exp(s - mx);
      std::vector<double> ctx(2, 0);
      for (int t = 0; t < 6; ++t) {
        const double w = std::exp(sc[t] - mx) / z;
        dw = std::max(dw, std::abs(w - a.weights.at({n, t})));
        for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i) ctx[i] += w * va.at({n, t, i});
      }
      for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i) dout = std::max(dout, std::abs(ctx[i] - a.output.at({n, i})));
    }
    std::print("Additive attention, q [2, 5], k [2, 6, 3], v [2, 6, 2], 4 units: weights {}, context {}, max differences from loops {:.1e} and {:.1e}, {} parameters (5·4 + 3·4 + 4).\n",
               nn::shape_str(a.weights.shape()), nn::shape_str(a.output.shape()), dw, dout, p.count());
  }

  // 3. Multi-head self-attention, x [2, 5, 16], 4 heads of 4, against a per-head computation.
  {
    nn::ParameterStore p(4, F64);
    const Tensor x = nn::normal({2, 5, 16}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    nn::MultiHeadOptions o;
    o.need_weights = true;
    const auto a = nn::multi_head_attention(p, "self", x, x, 4, o);
    const Tensor Wq = p.at("self/Wq"), Wk = p.at("self/Wk"), Wv = p.at("self/Wv"), Wo = p.at("self/Wo");
    double dmax = 0;
    for (int n = 0; n < 2; ++n) {
      const Tensor xn = nn::select(x, 0, n);
      std::vector<Tensor> heads;
      for (int h = 0; h < 4; ++h) {
        auto cols = [h](const Tensor& W) { return nn::slice(W, {0, 4 * h}, {16, 4}); };
        Rows w, out;
        attend(rows(nn::matmul(xn, cols(Wq))), rows(nn::matmul(xn, cols(Wk))), rows(nn::matmul(xn, cols(Wv))), [](auto, auto) { return true; }, w, out);
        std::vector<double> flat;
        for (auto& r : out) flat.insert(flat.end(), r.begin(), r.end());
        heads.push_back(nn::from_vector(flat, {5, 4}));
      }
      dmax = std::max(dmax, maxdiff(rows(nn::matmul(nn::concat(heads, 1), Wo)), nn::select(a.output, 0, n)));
    }
    std::print("Multi-head attention, x [2, 5, 16], 4 heads: output {}, weights {}, {} parameters (4·16·16), max difference from the per-head loops {:.1e}.\n",
               nn::shape_str(a.output.shape()), nn::shape_str(a.weights.shape()), p.count(), dmax);
    std::print("Head weights, example 0, query 2: ");
    for (int h = 0; h < 4; ++h) {
      std::print("head {} [", h);
      for (int j = 0; j < 5; ++j) std::print("{}{:.3f}", j ? " " : "", a.weights.at({0, h, 2, j}));
      std::print("]{}", h < 3 ? ", " : ".\n");
    }
  }

  // 4. The softmax Jacobian: d alpha / d s = diag(alpha) - alpha alpha^T, so dL/ds = alpha (r - alpha . r).
  {
    const Tensor s = nn::normal({5}, 0, 1, gen, F64).requires_grad_(true), r = nn::normal({5}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    const Tensor alpha = nn::softmax(s);
    const Tensor g = nn::grad({nn::sum(alpha * r)}, {s})[0];
    const double dot = nn::sum(alpha * r).item();
    double err = 0;
    for (int j = 0; j < 5; ++j) err = std::max(err, std::abs(alpha.at({j}) * (r.at({j}) - dot) - g.at({j})));
    std::print("Softmax gradient: max |autograd − α(r − α·r)| = {:.1e}; the gradient sums to {:.1e}.\n", err, nn::sum(g).item());
  }

  // Figure: attention weights of one random sequence (T = 12, d = 16) under four masks.
  const int T = 12;
  const Tensor qs = nn::normal({T, 16}, 0, 1, gen, F64), ks = nn::normal({T, 16}, 0, 1, gen, F64), vs = nn::normal({T, 1}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
  const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, Tensor>> masks = {{"No mask", Tensor()}, {"Causal", nn::causal_keep_mask(T)},
                                                             {"Causal window of 4", nn::local_keep_mask(T, T, 4)}, {"Two-sided window of 3", nn::local_keep_mask(T, T, 3, false)}};
  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  std::vector<std::string> ylab;
  for (int i = 0; i < T; ++i) xs.push_back(i), ys.push_back(i), ylab.push_back(std::to_string(T - 1 - i));
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  for (const auto& [title, mask] : masks) {
    const auto a = nn::scaled_dot_product_attention(qs, ks, vs, {.mask = mask, .need_weights = true});
    const auto e = nn::attention_entropy(a.weights);
    std::print("{}: mean entropy of a row {:.3f} nats.  ", title, nn::mean(e).item());
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(T, std::vector<double>(T));
    for (int j = 0; j < T; ++j)
      for (int i = 0; i < T; ++i) {
        const bool kept = !mask.defined() || mask.at({i, j}) != 0;
        z[j][T - 1 - i] = kept ? a.weights.at({i, j}) : std::nan("");
      }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::viridis_colors(32), 0.0, 1.0).x_limits(-0.5, T - 0.5).y_limits(-0.5, T - 0.5).title(title).x_label("key j").y_label("query i").y_tick_labels(ylab);
    fig.add(p);
  }
  std::print("\n");
  fig.size(1100, 1000).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/attention_masks.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| case | max \|weights − loops\| | max \|output − loops\| | weights (rows = queries) |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | :--- |
| plain | 5.6e-17 | 1.2e-16 | 0.578 0.146 0.247 0.030; 0.209 0.121 0.217 0.453; 0.755 0.036 0.067 0.142; 0.034 0.045 0.097 0.824 |
| causal | 0.0e+00 | 1.1e-16 | 1.000 0.000 0.000 0.000; 0.633 0.367 0.000 0.000; 0.880 0.042 0.078 0.000; 0.034 0.045 0.097 0.824 |
| local, window 2 | 0.0e+00 | 2.8e-17 | 1.000 0.000 0.000 0.000; 0.633 0.367 0.000 0.000; 0.000 0.354 0.646 0.000; 0.000 0.000 0.105 0.895 |

Fused causal kernel against the plain path: max difference 1.1e-16.
Additive attention, q [2, 5], k [2, 6, 3], v [2, 6, 2], 4 units: weights [2, 6], context [2, 2], max differences from loops 1.1e-16 and 1.1e-16, 36 parameters (5·4 + 3·4 + 4).
Multi-head attention, x [2, 5, 16], 4 heads: output [2, 5, 16], weights [2, 4, 5, 5], 1024 parameters (4·16·16), max difference from the per-head loops 4.4e-16.
Head weights, example 0, query 2: head 0 [0.369 0.122 0.200 0.231 0.078], head 1 [0.091 0.102 0.566 0.069 0.171], head 2 [0.438 0.085 0.009 0.309 0.158], head 3 [0.094 0.409 0.232 0.098 0.167].
Softmax gradient: max |autograd − α(r − α·r)| = 0.0e+00; the gradient sums to 3.5e-17.
No mask: mean entropy of a row 2.110 nats.  Causal: mean entropy of a row 1.364 nats.  Causal window of 4: mean entropy of a row 0.998 nats.  Two-sided window of 3: mean entropy of a row 1.195 nats.  
\image(figures/attention_masks.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Attention weights of one random sequence, 12 queries by 12 keys with d equals 16, under four masks. Colour runs from dark purple (weight 0) through green to yellow (weight 1). White cells are masked out.)
\alttext(Top left, no mask: a full 12 by 12 grid of mostly dark cells with a few brighter ones, such as query 3 on key 2 and query 9 on key 5. Top right, causal: the lower triangle including the diagonal; query 0 puts all its weight on key 0 (yellow), and later rows spread their weight. Bottom left, causal window of 4: a band of four cells ending on the diagonal. Bottom right, two-sided window of 3: a band of five cells centred on the diagonal, three at the first and last rows.)

All three scaled dot-product cases agree with the loops to within \(1.2\times10^{-16}\), as do the fused causal kernel against the plain path, additive attention, and multi-head attention against the per-head computation,
to within \(4.4\times10^{-16}\). Additive attention with 4 units has \(5\cdot4+3\cdot4+4=36\) parameters, and multi-head attention has \(4\cdot16^2=1024\), the same as a single head of width 16 would have. The causal rows
show the mask at work. Query 0 can see only key 0 and gives it weight 1. Query 3 sees every key, so its causal and plain weights coincide. With a window of 2, query 2 loses key 0 and renormalises over keys 1 and 2.
The four heads distribute query 2's weight very differently, which is the point of having several. The softmax gradient matches \(\alpha\odot(r-\alpha\cdot r)\) exactly and sums to \(3.5\times10^{-17}\).

In the figure, the mean entropy of a row falls from 2.110 nats without a mask to 1.364 under the causal mask, and to 0.998 and 1.195 with the windows. Restricting what a query
can see lowers its entropy, since fewer keys share the weight.

> Checking the theory

>> The scale, the entropy law, random features and cost

The next program draws random queries and 64 random keys and measures score variances, softmax entropies and Jacobian norms with and without the \(1/\sqrt d\) scale. It tests the entropy law
for 64 and 4096 keys, measures the error of FAVOR+ attention against exact softmax attention as the number of features grows, and times plain, fused and kernel attention for one head.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/attention_theory.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

// Entropy and Jacobian Frobenius norm of the softmax of one row of scores.
std::pair<double, double> softmax_stats(const std::vector<double>& s) {
  const double mx = std::ranges::max(s);
  double z = 0;
  for (double x : s) z += std::exp(x - mx);
  std::vector<double> p;
  double h = 0;
  for (double x : s) {
    p.push_back(std::exp(x - mx) / z);
    if (p.back() > 0) h -= p.back() * std::log(p.back());
  }
  // ||diag(p) - p p^T||_F^2, entry by entry (the closed form cancels badly when p is nearly one-hot).
  double f2 = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < p.size(); ++i) {
    double rest = 0;  // 1 - p_i, summed from the other entries
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < p.size(); ++j)
      if (j != i) rest += p[j], f2 += p[i] * p[i] * p[j] * p[j];
    f2 += p[i] * p[i] * rest * rest;
  }
  return {h, std::sqrt(f2)};
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::mt19937_64 rng(2);
  std::normal_distribution<double> normal(0, 1);
  const int n = 64, trials = 400;
  // 1. q, k with N(0, 1) entries: score variance, softmax entropy and Jacobian size, unscaled vs scaled.
  std::print("| d | var(q·k) | var(q·k/√d) | entropy, unscaled | entropy, scaled | ‖J‖_F, unscaled | ‖J‖_F, scaled |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int d : {4, 16, 64, 256, 1024}) {
    double vr = 0, vs = 0, hr = 0, hs = 0, jr = 0, js = 0;
    for (int t = 0; t < trials; ++t) {
      std::vector<double> q(d), raw(n), scaled(n);
      for (double& x : q) x = normal(rng);
      for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
        double s = 0;
        for (int a = 0; a < d; ++a) s += q[a] * normal(rng);
        raw[j] = s, scaled[j] = s / std::sqrt(double(d));
        vr += s * s / (n * trials), vs += s * s / d / (n * trials);
      }
      const auto [h1, j1] = softmax_stats(raw);
      const auto [h2, j2] = softmax_stats(scaled);
      hr += h1 / trials, hs += h2 / trials, jr += j1 / trials, js += j2 / trials;
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.1f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", d, vr, vs, hr, hs, jr, js);
  }
  // 2. Entropy against the score scale sigma, for 64 iid N(0, sigma^2) scores, with log n - sigma^2 / 2.
  const double sigma_c = std::sqrt(2 * std::log(double(n)));
  std::vector<double> sig, ent, jac, pred;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 32; ++i) {
    const double sg = 0.375 * i;
    double h = 0, jn = 0;
    for (int t = 0; t < trials; ++t) {
      std::vector<double> s(n);
      for (double& x : s) x = sg * normal(rng);
      const auto [a, b] = softmax_stats(s);
      h += a / trials, jn += b / trials;
    }
    sig.push_back(sg), ent.push_back(h), jac.push_back(jn), pred.push_back(std::max(0.0, std::log(double(n)) - sg * sg / 2));
  }
  std::print("\nlog 64 = {:.4f}; threshold sqrt(2 log 64) = {:.3f}.\n", std::log(64.0), sigma_c);
  for (int i : {2, 4, 6, 8, 12, 16, 24, 32})
    std::print("sigma {}: entropy {:.3f} (law {:.3f}), Jacobian norm {:.3f}{}", sig[i], ent[i], pred[i], jac[i], i < 32 ? "; " : ".\n");
  const auto peak = std::ranges::max_element(jac) - jac.begin();
  std::print("Largest mean Jacobian norm {:.3f}, at sigma = {}.\n", jac[peak], sig[peak]);

  // The law is the large-n limit: repeat a few scales with n = 4096 keys.
  std::print("With n = 4096 keys (log n = {:.3f}, threshold {:.3f}): ", std::log(4096.0), std::sqrt(2 * std::log(4096.0)));
  for (double sg : {0.75, 1.5, 2.25, 3.0}) {
    double h = 0;
    for (int t = 0; t < 50; ++t) {
      std::vector<double> s(4096);
      for (double& x : s) x = sg * normal(rng);
      const double mx = std::ranges::max(s);
      double z = 0, e = 0;
      for (double x : s) z += std::exp(x - mx);
      for (double x : s) { const double p = std::exp(x - mx) / z; if (p > 0) e -= p * std::log(p); }
      h += e / 50;
    }
    std::print("sigma {}: {:.3f} (law {:.3f}){}", sg, h, std::log(4096.0) - sg * sg / 2, sg < 3 ? ", " : ".\n");
  }

  // 3. FAVOR+: relative error of random-feature attention against exact softmax attention, against m.
  nn::Generator gen(3);
  const int T = 64, d = 16;
  const Tensor q = nn::normal({T, d}, 0, 0.5, gen, F64), k = nn::normal({T, d}, 0, 0.5, gen, F64), v = nn::normal({T, 4}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
  const Tensor exact = nn::scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v).output;
  const Tensor exact_w = nn::scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, {.need_weights = true}).weights;
  std::vector<double> ms, e_iid, e_orth;
  std::print("\n| m | relative error, iid features | orthogonal features | error × √m (iid) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int m = 16; m <= 4096; m *= 2) {
    double a = 0, b = 0;
    const int reps = 20;
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
      for (int orth = 0; orth < 2; ++orth) {
        const Tensor w = orth ? nn::orthogonal_gaussian_projection(m, d, gen, F64) : nn::normal({m, d}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
        const Tensor out = nn::kernel_attention(nn::positive_random_features(q, w), nn::positive_random_features(k, w), v, false, 0.0);
        const double err = std::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(out - exact)).item() / nn::sum(nn::square(exact)).item());
        (orth ? b : a) += err / reps;
      }
    }
    ms.push_back(m), e_iid.push_back(a), e_orth.push_back(b);
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.3f} |\n", m, a, b, a * std::sqrt(m));
  }
  // The kernel estimate is unbiased: average phi(q).phi(k) over many draws for one pair, against exp(q.k / sqrt d).
  {
    const Tensor qi = nn::slice(q, {0, 0}, {1, d}), kj = nn::slice(k, {1, 0}, {1, d});
    const double target = std::exp(nn::sum(qi * kj).item() / std::sqrt(double(d)));
    const Tensor w = nn::normal({1 << 20, d}, 0, 1, gen, F64);
    const double est = nn::sum(nn::positive_random_features(qi, w) * nn::positive_random_features(kj, w)).item();
    std::print("\nOne pair, 2^20 features: estimate {:.5f}, exp(q·k/√d) = {:.5f}.\n", est, target);
  }

  // 4. Cost against T (d = 64, one head, float32): softmax (plain and fused) against kernel attention with elu+1 features.
  std::print("\n| T | plain softmax (ms) | fused softmax (ms) | kernel attention (ms) | plain / kernel |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> Ts, tp, tf, tk;
  for (int Tn : {256, 512, 1024, 2048, 4096}) {
    const Tensor Q = nn::normal({Tn, 64}, 0, 1, gen), K = nn::normal({Tn, 64}, 0, 1, gen), V = nn::normal({Tn, 64}, 0, 1, gen);
    auto best = [](auto&& f) {
      double b = 1e300;
      for (int r = 0; r < 7; ++r) {
        const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
        f();
        b = std::min(b, std::chrono::duration<double, std::milli>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count());
      }
      return b;
    };
    nn::NoGradGuard ng;
    const double a = best([&] { (void)nn::scaled_dot_product_attention(Q, K, V, {.impl = nn::AttentionImpl::Plain}).output; });
    const double b = best([&] { (void)nn::scaled_dot_product_attention(Q, K, V, {.impl = nn::AttentionImpl::Fused}).output; });
    const double c = best([&] { (void)nn::kernel_attention(nn::elu_feature_map(Q), nn::elu_feature_map(K), V); });
    Ts.push_back(Tn), tp.push_back(a), tf.push_back(b), tk.push_back(c);
    std::print("| {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.1f} |\n", Tn, a, b, c, a / c);
  }
  auto slope = [](const std::vector<double>& x, const std::vector<double>& y) {
    return (std::log(y.back()) - std::log(y[y.size() - 2])) / (std::log(x.back()) - std::log(x[x.size() - 2]));
  };
  std::print("\nLog-log slopes between T = 2048 and 4096: plain {:.2f}, fused {:.2f}, kernel {:.2f}.\n", slope(Ts, tp), slope(Ts, tf), slope(Ts, tk));

  // Figure.
  auto lg = [](const std::vector<double>& x) { std::vector<double> y; for (double v : x) y.push_back(std::log10(v)); return y; };
  const dm::graphics::RGB blue{37, 99, 235}, orange{234, 88, 12}, green{22, 163, 74}, grey{148, 163, 184};
  auto p1 = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p1.lines(sig, pred, "log n − σ²/2", grey, 3.0).points(sig, ent, blue, 3.5)
      .lines({1.0, 1.0}, {0.0, 4.5}, "σ = 1 (scaled)", green, 1.5).lines({sigma_c, sigma_c}, {0.0, 4.5}, "σ = √(2 log n)", orange, 1.5)
      .x_limits(0, 12).y_limits(0, 6).title("Softmax entropy, 64 keys").x_label("score standard deviation σ").y_label("entropy (nats)");
  auto p2 = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p2.points(sig, jac, blue, 3.5).lines({1.0, 1.0}, {0.0, 0.25}, "σ = 1 (scaled)", green, 1.5).lines({sigma_c, sigma_c}, {0.0, 0.25}, "σ = √(2 log n)", orange, 1.5)
      .x_limits(0, 12).y_limits(0, 0.5).title("Size of the softmax Jacobian").x_label("score standard deviation σ").y_label("mean ‖diag(α) − αα^T‖_F");
  auto p3 = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> ref;
  for (double m : ms) ref.push_back(e_iid.front() * std::sqrt(ms.front() / m));
  p3.lines(lg(ms), lg(ref), "∝ m^(−1/2)", grey, 3.0).lines(lg(ms), lg(e_iid), "iid features", blue, 2.0).points(lg(ms), lg(e_iid), blue, 3.0)
      .lines(lg(ms), lg(e_orth), "orthogonal features", orange, 2.0).points(lg(ms), lg(e_orth), orange, 3.0)
      .x_limits(1.1, 3.7).y_limits(-1.25, 0.75).title("FAVOR+ error against exact attention").x_label("log10 number of features m").y_label("log10 relative error");
  auto p4 = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p4.lines(lg(Ts), lg(tp), "plain softmax", blue, 2.0).points(lg(Ts), lg(tp), blue, 3.0).lines(lg(Ts), lg(tf), "fused softmax", orange, 2.0).points(lg(Ts), lg(tf), orange, 3.0)
      .lines(lg(Ts), lg(tk), "kernel (elu + 1)", green, 2.0).points(lg(Ts), lg(tk), green, 3.0)
      .x_limits(2.3, 3.7).y_limits(-1.5, 3).title("Time for one head, d = 64").x_label("log10 sequence length T").y_label("log10 milliseconds (best of 7)");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  fig.add(p1).add(p2).add(p3).add(p4);
  fig.size(1100, 840).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/attention_theory.svg");
}
```
