// result_end

\caption(Top left: mean entropy of the softmax of 64 independent normal scores against their standard deviation sigma (points), with the large-n law log n minus sigma squared over 2 (grey). Top right: the mean Frobenius norm of the softmax Jacobian. Bottom left: relative error of FAVOR+ attention against exact softmax attention for 64 queries and keys with d equals 16, mean of 20 draws, against the number of features. Bottom right: time for one head with d equals 64 against sequence length, best of 7 runs, on log scales.)
\alttext(Top left: entropy falls from 4.16 at sigma 0, following the grey law closely up to about sigma 1.5; the law then drops to 0 at the orange line, sigma 2.88, while the points decline more slowly, to about 1.8 at sigma 3 and 0.4 at sigma 12. A green line marks sigma 1. Top right: the Jacobian norm rises from 0.12 at sigma 0 to a peak of about 0.31 near sigma 2.6, then slowly falls to about 0.16 at sigma 12. Bottom left: both error curves fall almost in parallel with the grey reference line of slope minus one half, from about 1.5 at 16 features to about 0.12 at 4096, the orthogonal curve slightly lower. Bottom right: the plain softmax line rises steeply; the fused line is lowest at short lengths and rises less steeply; the kernel line is nearly flat, crossing the fused line between log10 T of 3 and 3.3.)

*The scale.* Unscaled score variances are within 3% of \(d\) and scaled ones within 1.5% of 1. Without the scale, the entropy over 64 keys falls from 2.770 nats at \(d=4\) to 0.100 at \(d=1024\), where the
weight sits on about \(e^{0.100}\approx1.1\) keys. With the scale it stays at 3.68 to 3.69 for every \(d\), within 1% of the law's \(\log64-1/2=3.659\). The Jacobian, however, does not simply shrink as the softmax sharpens.
Its norm is 0.18 with the scale, larger without it at \(d=4\) and 16 (0.258 and 0.290), and smaller only from \(d=256\) (0.052 at \(d=1024\)). The figure shows why. The norm rises from \(\sqrt{63}/64=0.124\) for uniform weights to a peak of 0.306 near
\(\sigma=\) 2.6, where two or three keys share the weight, and declines only slowly beyond. The case for the scale is therefore not that unscaled gradients vanish at moderate \(d\). It is that unscaled attention starts frozen
onto whichever keys happen to score highest, with a low entropy that bears no relation to the task (0.59 nats at \(d=64\), against 3.69 with the scale), and its weights only become insensitive to the scores at very large \(d\).

*The entropy law.* For 64 keys the law is within 0.3% at \(\sigma=0.75\) and 5% at \(\sigma=1.5\), but at \(\sigma=2.25\) the measured entropy is 2.434 against the law's 1.628. Past the threshold \(\sigma=2.88\) the
entropy keeps falling smoothly instead of reaching zero (1.762 at \(\sigma=3\)). With 4096 keys the law holds further out: it agrees to 0.1% up to \(\sigma=1.5\), and to 2.4% at \(\sigma=2.25\). Like the random energy model's transition, the collapse is
sharp only as \(n\to\infty\), and with 64 keys finite-size effects blur it.

*Random features.* One pair of vectors estimated with \(2^{20}\) features gives 1.12256 against an exact 1.12227, as unbiasedness predicts. For whole attention outputs the relative error falls from 1.46 at 16 features to
0.128 at 4096. The product error\(\cdot\sqrt m\) grows from 5.9 and levels off near 8.2 from \(m=2048\), the \(m^{-1/2}\) rate of the variance formula. Orthogonal features are more accurate at every \(m\), by 2% to 17%. Even
for these small vectors (entries with standard deviation 0.5), 4096 features, 256 times the key dimension, still leave an error of 11% to 13%.

*Cost.* Plain softmax attention grows like \(T^2\), with a log-log slope of 2.23 between 2048 and 4096 tokens. Kernel attention grows far more slowly, with slope 0.36, since at these sizes fixed overheads still dominate its
linear cost. The fused softmax kernel is the fastest up to 1024 tokens, and kernel attention overtakes it between 1024 and 2048. At 4096 tokens kernel attention takes 1.38 ms, 49 times less than plain softmax attention
(67.4 ms) and 6 times less than the fused kernel (8.82 ms). Timings vary between runs; another run gave a factor of 40 against plain attention.

> Training: a lookup task

The original example's task: each sequence holds 6 (key id, value) pairs, with distinct ids from 10 and standard-normal values. Given a query id, the model must output the value stored under it. The only
parameters are a \(10\times8\) table of embeddings, used for both queries and keys. The prediction is the attention-weighted average of the 6 values, so a perfect model puts all its weight on the matching key.
Training is 600 Adam steps with learning rate 0.02 and batches of 64, evaluated on 512 held-out queries. The program trains the same model with three kinds of attention: softmax, elu-kernel, and FAVOR+ with 128 fixed orthogonal features.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/attention_lookup.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

// Lookup: 6 (key id, value) pairs and a query id; predict the value stored under the query's key.
struct Batch { Tensor keys, query, values, target; };
Batch make(int n, std::mt19937_64& rng) {
  const int T = 6, ids = 10;
  std::normal_distribution<double> normal(0, 1);
  std::vector<std::int64_t> kv(n * T), qv(n);
  std::vector<double> vv(n * T), tv(n);
  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    std::vector<int> perm(ids);
    std::iota(perm.begin(), perm.end(), 0);
    std::shuffle(perm.begin(), perm.end(), rng);  // distinct keys
    const int which = static_cast<int>(rng() % T);
    for (int t = 0; t < T; ++t) kv[i * T + t] = perm[t], vv[i * T + t] = normal(rng);
    qv[i] = perm[which], tv[i] = vv[i * T + which];
  }
  return {nn::from_vector(kv, {n, T}), nn::from_vector(qv, {n}), nn::from_vector(vv, {n, T}), nn::from_vector(tv, {n})};
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const int T = 6, ids = 10, dim = 8, steps = 600;
  enum Kind { Softmax, Elu, Favor };
  const std::vector<std::pair<Kind, std::string>> kinds = {{Softmax, "softmax"}, {Elu, "kernel, elu + 1"}, {Favor, "FAVOR+, m = 128"}};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> curves;
  std::vector<int> checkpoints;
  std::string note;
  std::print("| attention | test MSE, step 1 | step 100 | step 300 | step 600 | weight on the matching key, step 600 | pointer accuracy |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [kind, name] : kinds) {
    nn::ParameterStore p(5, F64);
    const Tensor table = p.get("emb", {ids, dim}, nn::init::uniform(0.5));
    nn::Generator fg(11);
    const Tensor w = nn::orthogonal_gaussian_projection(128, dim, fg, F64);  // fixed FAVOR+ projection
    nn::Optimizer adam(p, 0.02, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
    std::mt19937_64 rng(5);
    // Returns the prediction [B] and the attention weights [B, T].
    auto forward = [&](const Batch& b) {
      const Tensor k = nn::gather(table, b.keys, 0);                       // [B, T, dim]
      const Tensor q = nn::expand_dims(nn::gather(table, b.query, 0), 1);  // [B, 1, dim]
      Tensor wts;
      if (kind == Softmax) {
        wts = nn::softmax(nn::squeeze(nn::matmul(q, nn::swapaxes(k, -2, -1)), {1}) / std::sqrt(double(dim)), -1);
      } else {
        const Tensor pq = kind == Elu ? nn::elu_feature_map(q) : nn::positive_random_features(q, w);
        const Tensor pk = kind == Elu ? nn::elu_feature_map(k) : nn::positive_random_features(k, w);
        const Tensor raw = nn::squeeze(nn::matmul(pq, nn::swapaxes(pk, -2, -1)), {1});  // [B, T]
        wts = raw / nn::sum(raw, {-1}, true);
      }
      return std::make_pair(nn::sum(wts * b.values, {-1}), wts);
    };
    const Batch test = make(512, rng);
    std::vector<double> mse_at, on_target;
    double final_on = 0, acc = 0;
    for (int step = 1; step <= steps; ++step) {
      const Batch b = make(64, rng);
      adam.step(nn::grad({nn::mean(nn::square(forward(b).first - b.target))}, {table}));
      if (step == 1 || step % 25 == 0) {
        nn::NoGradGuard ng;
        const auto [pred, wts] = forward(test);
        const Tensor match = nn::cast(nn::equal(test.keys, nn::expand_dims(test.query, 1)), F64);
        const double on = nn::mean(nn::sum(wts * match, {-1})).item();
        if (kind == Softmax) checkpoints.push_back(step);
        on_target.push_back(on);
        if (step == 1 || step == 100 || step == 300 || step == 600) mse_at.push_back(nn::mean(nn::square(pred - test.target)).item());
        if (step == steps) {
          final_on = on;
          acc = nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(wts, -1), nn::argmax(match, -1)), F64)).item();
        }
      }
    }
    curves.push_back(on_target);
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.3f} | {:.1f}% |\n", name, mse_at[0], mse_at[1], mse_at[2], mse_at[3], final_on, 100 * acc);
    if (kind == Softmax) {
      // The learned embeddings: cosine similarity between distinct ids, and the score gap that sharpens the pointer.
      nn::NoGradGuard ng;
      const Tensor e = table / nn::sqrt(nn::sum(nn::square(table), {-1}, true));
      const Tensor cos = nn::matmul(e, nn::swapaxes(e, 0, 1));
      const Tensor gram = nn::matmul(table, nn::swapaxes(table, 0, 1)) / std::sqrt(double(dim));
      double off = -1, self_min = 1e300, gap = 1e300;
      for (int i = 0; i < ids; ++i) {
        self_min = std::min(self_min, gram.at({i, i}));
        for (int j = 0; j < ids; ++j)
          if (i != j) off = std::max(off, cos.at({i, j})), gap = std::min(gap, gram.at({i, i}) - gram.at({i, j}));
      }
      note = std::format("\nSoftmax model: largest cosine between distinct embeddings {:.3f}; smallest scaled self-score {:.2f}; smallest gap between the matching score and any other {:.2f}, so the matching key gets at least 1/(1 + 5 e^-gap) = {:.3f} of the weight.\n",
                 off, self_min, gap, 1 / (1 + 5 * std::exp(-gap)));
    }
  }
  std::print("{}", note);
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  std::vector<double> xs(checkpoints.begin(), checkpoints.end());
  const dm::graphics::RGB cols[] = {{37, 99, 235}, {234, 88, 12}, {22, 163, 74}};
  for (std::size_t c = 0; c < curves.size(); ++c) plot.lines(xs, curves[c], kinds[c].second, cols[c], 2.0);
  plot.lines({0.0, double(steps)}, {1.0 / T, 1.0 / T}, "1/6 (uniform)", dm::graphics::RGB{148, 163, 184}, 1.0);
  plot.x_limits(0, steps).y_limits(0, 1.5).title("Lookup task: attention on the matching key").x_label("Adam step (batches of 64)").y_label("mean weight on matching key");
  plot.size(1100, 440).save("build/attention_lookup.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| attention | test MSE, step 1 | step 100 | step 300 | step 600 | weight on the matching key, step 600 | pointer accuracy |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| softmax | 0.7056 | 0.0035 | 0.0005 | 0.0002 | 0.989 | 100.0% |
| kernel, elu + 1 | 0.7601 | 0.6054 | 0.1775 | 0.1264 | 0.811 | 88.1% |
| FAVOR+, m = 128 | 0.7056 | 0.0125 | 0.0013 | 0.0004 | 0.983 | 100.0% |

Softmax model: largest cosine between distinct embeddings 0.208; smallest scaled self-score 5.61; smallest gap between the matching score and any other 4.90, so the matching key gets at least 1/(1 + 5 e^-gap) = 0.964 of the weight.
\image(figures/attention_lookup.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Mean attention weight on the matching key over 512 test queries during training, for softmax, elu-kernel and FAVOR+ attention, with the uniform weight one sixth (grey).)
\alttext(All three curves start near 0.17 to 0.2. The blue softmax curve rises steeply to about 0.92 by step 75 and 0.95 by step 100, then creeps up to 0.99. The green FAVOR+ curve follows just below it, reaching 0.98. The orange elu-kernel curve rises slowly, passing 0.5 near step 210 and levelling off at about 0.81.)

Softmax attention reproduces the original example's TensorFlow result: test MSE 0.0002 and weight 0.989 on the matching key, with the argmax picking the right key for all 512 queries. The learned embeddings are nearly orthogonal (largest
cosine between distinct ids 0.208), and every matching score beats every other by at least 4.90. That margin alone guarantees weight \(1/(1+5e^{-\text{gap}})=\) 0.964 to the matching key. FAVOR+ attention learns
almost as well (MSE 0.0004, weight 0.983), since its features estimate the same exponential kernel. Elu-kernel attention reaches only 0.811 on the matching key and 88.1% pointer accuracy, with MSE 0.126.
Its weights are proportional to \((\mathrm{elu}(q)+1)\cdot(\mathrm{elu}(k)+1)\), which grows only linearly in each embedding, so a sharp pointer needs embeddings that are both large and nearly
disjoint in their positive parts. The exponential makes sharpness cheap, and that is what linear attention gives up.

> A dataset: digits as sequences

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) give 1797 handwritten digits as \(8\times8\) images with grey levels 0 to 16. Here each image becomes a sequence of 8 tokens, its pixel rows, scaled to \([0,1]\) and centred.
The model embeds each row linearly into 32 dimensions, adds a learned position embedding, applies one residual multi-head self-attention block (4 heads of 8), averages over the tokens and classifies linearly.
To keep the run short it trains in single precision on the first 600 digits with 100 full-batch Adam steps (learning rate 0.01) for three seeds, and tests on the last 360.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/attention_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
namespace init = dm::nn::init;
using nn::Tensor;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // Each digit is a sequence of 8 tokens, its pixel rows (8 grey levels each, scaled to [0, 1] and centred).
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, n_train = 600, n_test_from = 1437, T = 8, D = 32, H = 4;
  std::vector<double> v(static_cast<std::size_t>(n * 64));
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < 64; ++j) {
    const auto c = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
    const double m = std::accumulate(c.begin(), c.end(), 0.0) / n / 16.0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) v[i * 64 + j] = c[i] / 16.0 - m;
  }
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(v, {n, T, 8}).to(nn::DType::Float32);
  const Tensor y = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(digits.get_column<double>("digit").values(), {n}), nn::DType::Int64);
  const Tensor Xtr = nn::slice(X, {0, 0, 0}, {n_train, T, 8}), Xte = nn::slice(X, {n_test_from, 0, 0}, {n - n_test_from, T, 8});
  const Tensor ytr = nn::slice(y, {0}, {n_train}), yte = nn::slice(y, {n_test_from}, {n - n_test_from});

  // Token embedding 8 -> 32 plus a learned position embedding, one residual multi-head self-attention block
  // (4 heads of 8), mean over the tokens, and a linear classifier. 100 full-batch Adam steps on the first
  // 600 digits (single precision, to keep the run short), three seeds; the last 360 digits test.
  enum Kind { None, Softmax, Local, Kernel };
  const std::vector<std::pair<Kind, std::string>> kinds = {
      {None, "no attention"}, {Softmax, "softmax"}, {Local, "softmax, two-sided window of 3"}, {Kernel, "kernel, elu + 1"}};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> pattern(T, std::vector<double>(T, 0));
  std::vector<double> head_entropy(H, 0);
  std::print("| attention | train loss, step 100 | test accuracy, mean of 3 seeds | min–max over seeds |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& [kind, name] : kinds) {
    std::vector<double> accs, losses;
    for (std::uint64_t seed : {1, 2, 3}) {
      nn::ParameterStore p(seed, nn::DType::Float32);
      const Tensor We = p.get("We", {8, D}, init::glorot_uniform()), P = p.get("pos", {T, D}, init::normal(0.1));
      const Tensor Wc = p.get("Wc", {D, 10}, init::glorot_uniform());
      std::vector<Tensor> params = {We, P, Wc};
      Tensor Wq, Wk, Wv, Wo;
      if (kind != None) {
        Wq = p.get("Wq", {D, D}, init::glorot_uniform()), Wk = p.get("Wk", {D, D}, init::glorot_uniform());
        Wv = p.get("Wv", {D, D}, init::glorot_uniform()), Wo = p.get("Wo", {D, D}, init::glorot_uniform());
        params.insert(params.end(), {Wq, Wk, Wv, Wo});
      }
      nn::Optimizer opt(p, 0.01, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
      const Tensor window = nn::local_keep_mask(T, T, 3, false);
      auto forward = [&](const Tensor& x, Tensor* weights) {
        Tensor h = nn::matmul(x, We) + P;  // [N, T, D]
        if (kind != None) {
          const Tensor q = nn::split_heads(nn::matmul(h, Wq), H), k = nn::split_heads(nn::matmul(h, Wk), H), val = nn::split_heads(nn::matmul(h, Wv), H);
          Tensor o;
          if (kind == Kernel) o = nn::kernel_attention(nn::elu_feature_map(q), nn::elu_feature_map(k), val);
          else {
            const auto a = nn::scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, val, {.mask = kind == Local ? window : Tensor(), .need_weights = weights != nullptr});
            o = a.output;
            if (weights) *weights = a.weights;
          }
          h = h + nn::matmul(nn::merge_heads(o), Wo);
        }
        return nn::matmul(nn::mean(h, {1}), Wc);
      };
      double loss = 0;
      for (int s = 0; s < 100; ++s) {
        const Tensor l = nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(forward(Xtr, nullptr), ytr);
        opt.step(nn::grad({l}, params));
        loss = l.item();
      }
      nn::NoGradGuard ng;
      Tensor w;
      const Tensor logits = forward(Xte, kind == Softmax ? &w : nullptr);
      accs.push_back(nn::mean(nn::cast(nn::equal(nn::argmax(logits, -1), yte), F64)).item());
      losses.push_back(loss);
      if (kind == Softmax && seed == 1) {
        const Tensor avg = nn::mean(w, {0, 1});  // [T, T], over test digits and heads
        for (int i = 0; i < T; ++i) for (int j = 0; j < T; ++j) pattern[i][j] = avg.at({i, j});
        const Tensor e = nn::mean(nn::attention_entropy(w), {0, 2});  // [H]
        for (int h = 0; h < H; ++h) head_entropy[h] = e.at({h});
      }
    }
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.1f}% | {:.1f}–{:.1f}% |\n", name, (losses[0] + losses[1] + losses[2]) / 3, 100 * (accs[0] + accs[1] + accs[2]) / 3,
               100 * std::ranges::min(accs), 100 * std::ranges::max(accs));
  }
  std::print("\nSoftmax model, seed 1: mean entropy of each head's rows {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f} nats (log 8 = {:.3f}).\n", head_entropy[0], head_entropy[1],
             head_entropy[2], head_entropy[3], std::log(8.0));
  std::print("Mean weight on the query's own row {:.3f}; on rows 0 and 7 together {:.3f}.\n",
             [&] { double s = 0; for (int i = 0; i < T; ++i) s += pattern[i][i]; return s / T; }(),
             [&] { double s = 0; for (int i = 0; i < T; ++i) s += pattern[i][0] + pattern[i][7]; return s / T; }());

  std::vector<double> xs, ys;
  std::vector<std::string> ylab;
  for (int i = 0; i < T; ++i) xs.push_back(i), ys.push_back(i), ylab.push_back(std::format("row {}", T - 1 - i));
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> z(T, std::vector<double>(T));
  double zmax = 0;
  for (int j = 0; j < T; ++j) for (int i = 0; i < T; ++i) z[j][T - 1 - i] = pattern[i][j], zmax = std::max(zmax, pattern[i][j]);
  // How much each pixel row separates the classes: between-class share of its pixels' variance (training digits).
  std::vector<double> received(T, 0), separation(T, 0);
  {
    const auto lab = digits.get_column<double>("digit").values();
    for (int r = 0; r < T; ++r) {
      double between = 0, total = 0;
      for (int c = 0; c < 8; ++c) {
        const int j = r * 8 + c;
        std::vector<double> sum(10, 0), cnt(10, 0);
        double mean = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n_train; ++i) sum[int(lab[i])] += v[i * 64 + j], cnt[int(lab[i])] += 1, mean += v[i * 64 + j] / n_train;
        for (int i = 0; i < n_train; ++i) total += (v[i * 64 + j] - mean) * (v[i * 64 + j] - mean);
        for (int k = 0; k < 10; ++k) between += cnt[k] * (sum[k] / cnt[k] - mean) * (sum[k] / cnt[k] - mean);
      }
      separation[r] = total > 0 ? between / total : 0;
      for (int i = 0; i < T; ++i) received[r] += pattern[i][r] / T;
    }
  }
  const double mr = std::accumulate(received.begin(), received.end(), 0.0) / T, ms = std::accumulate(separation.begin(), separation.end(), 0.0) / T;
  double sxy = 0, sxx = 0, syy = 0;
  for (int r = 0; r < T; ++r) sxy += (received[r] - mr) * (separation[r] - ms), sxx += (received[r] - mr) * (received[r] - mr), syy += (separation[r] - ms) * (separation[r] - ms);
  std::print("\n| pixel row | mean attention received | between-class share of variance |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int r = 0; r < T; ++r) std::print("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |\n", r, received[r], separation[r]);
  std::print("\nCorrelation over the 8 rows: {:.2f}.\n", sxy / std::sqrt(sxx * syy));

  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.image(xs, ys, z, dm::graphics::viridis_colors(32), 0.0, zmax).x_limits(-0.5, T - 0.5).y_limits(-0.5, T - 0.5).y_tick_labels(ylab)
      .title("Mean attention weights (softmax)").x_label("key: pixel row").y_label("query");
  auto p2 = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  for (int r = 0; r < T; ++r) p2.text(separation[r] + 0.022, received[r], std::format("row {}", r));
  p2.points(separation, received, dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 5.0).x_limits(0.3, 0.55).y_limits(0, 0.3)
      .title("Attention received against class separation").x_label("between-class share of the row's pixel variance").y_label("mean attention received");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(p).add(p2);
  fig.size(1100, 500).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/attention_digits.svg");
  std::print("Largest mean weight {:.3f}.\n", zmax);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| attention | train loss, step 100 | test accuracy, mean of 3 seeds | min–max over seeds |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| no attention | 1.1708 | 49.2% | 48.3–50.0% |
| softmax | 0.0011 | 81.3% | 79.7–83.6% |
| softmax, two-sided window of 3 | 0.0011 | 80.3% | 79.7–81.1% |
| kernel, elu + 1 | 0.0024 | 84.4% | 83.6–85.6% |

Softmax model, seed 1: mean entropy of each head's rows 0.676, 0.779, 0.631, 0.731 nats (log 8 = 2.079).
Mean weight on the query's own row 0.116; on rows 0 and 7 together 0.132.

| pixel row | mean attention received | between-class share of variance |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 0.028 | 0.331 |
| 1 | 0.065 | 0.368 |
| 2 | 0.219 | 0.434 |
| 3 | 0.192 | 0.503 |
| 4 | 0.146 | 0.501 |
| 5 | 0.208 | 0.522 |
| 6 | 0.037 | 0.400 |
| 7 | 0.103 | 0.473 |

Correlation over the 8 rows: 0.78.
Largest mean weight 0.276.
\image(figures/attention_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: attention weights of the softmax model (seed 1), averaged over the 360 test digits and the 4 heads; rows are the query's pixel row, columns the key's. Colour runs from dark purple (0) to yellow (the largest mean weight, 0.276). Right: the mean attention each pixel row receives against how strongly its pixels separate the ten digits, measured as the between-class share of their variance in the training data.)
\alttext(Left: columns 2 to 5 are bright for most query rows, column 2 brightest for query rows 0 and 7 and column 5 for query rows 2 and 4; columns 0 and 6 are dark throughout. Right: eight labelled points rising from lower left to upper right; row 0 has the lowest separation, 0.33, and receives almost nothing; rows 1 and 6 receive under 0.07; row 2 receives the most, 0.22, at separation 0.43; rows 3, 4 and 5 have separations of 0.50 to 0.52 and receive 0.15 to 0.21; row 7 receives 0.10 at separation 0.47.)

Without attention the model sees very little. The mean of linearly embedded rows depends only on the mean row, the 8 column averages, so the model reaches 49.2% on the test set. One attention block lets each row's
representation depend on the others, and every attention variant fits the 600 training digits (loss 0.0024 or less) and reaches 80.3% to 84.4% mean test accuracy. Elu-kernel attention is ahead here (83.6% to 85.6% over seeds, against 79.7% to 83.6% for both softmax variants), but the ranges touch and three seeds of a 100-step fit are too few to rank the variants
firmly. With 8 tokens the quadratic cost is irrelevant, and without regularisation all of them overfit.

The softmax heads are sharp: their rows have mean entropies of 0.63 to 0.78 nats, against \(\log8=2.08\) for uniform weights. On average they attend most to pixel rows 2, 5 and 3 (mean weight 0.219, 0.208 and 0.192
received) and least to row 0 (0.028). Row 0 also separates the digits least. Across the 8 rows attention received correlates clearly with class separation (\(r=\) 0.78), but not perfectly: row 2 receives the most while separating the digits less than rows 3, 4 and 5.
Attention concentrates on informative rows without ranking them exactly by how informative they are.

> Conclusion

Attention is a soft dictionary lookup. datamunge's implementations agree with the definitions to rounding error, and the fused kernel agrees with the plain one. The \(1/\sqrt d\) scale keeps random scores at
unit variance. The large-\(n\) law \(H\approx\log n-\sigma^2/2\) shows what goes wrong without it: past \(\sigma=\sqrt{2\log n}\) the softmax freezes onto a few keys before learning starts. With finite \(n\) the law blurs, and
the Jacobian is largest when a few keys share the weight. At moderate \(d\), the cost of skipping the scale is arbitrary, frozen attention rather than vanishing gradients.

Kernel attention removes the quadratic cost exactly, by summing over keys once. FAVOR+ supplies positive random features whose inner products estimate the softmax kernel without bias, with error falling
like \(m^{-1/2}\). The price is accuracy. FAVOR+ needs thousands of features for 10% error even in 16 dimensions, and the cheap elu feature map defines a different, flatter kernel that could not learn a sharp
pointer in the lookup task. Kernel attention was more than 40 times faster than plain softmax attention at 4096 tokens, while the fused softmax kernel remained competitive up to about 1000 tokens. On digits read row by row,
one attention block lifted test accuracy from 49% to 80%–84%, attending mostly to informative middle pixel rows.

